---
// The three product stories: discover, build, prepare. Each carries an
// honest status line — all three lanes are planned work, not shipped
// capability (see docs/feature-ledger.md).
---

<section class="section stories" id="stories" aria-labelledby="stories-title">
  <div class="shell">
    <p class="kicker-inline">How Caddie works</p>
    <h2 id="stories-title">Three stories, one evidence trail</h2>

    <div class="story-grid">
      <article>
        <p class="story-idx">01</p>
        <h3>Discover opportunities</h3>
        <p>
          Caddie scouts open contests and turns them into candidates you can
          actually check: sources captured with provenance, rules quoted from
          immutable snapshots, deadlines normalized — and unsupported
          interpretations left unknown instead of guessed.
        </p>
        <p class="story-status">Planned · discovery &amp; evidence</p>
      </article>

      <article>
        <p class="story-idx">02</p>
        <h3>Build through specialists</h3>
        <p>
          A coordinator routes work through product, design, tech-lead,
          frontend, backend, mobile and QA specialists in one isolated
          sandbox — inside approved boundaries, under explicit budgets, with
          bounded repair and honest progress.
        </p>
        <p class="story-status">Planned · agent runtime</p>
      </article>

      <article>
        <p class="story-idx">03</p>
        <h3>Prepare verifiable submissions</h3>
        <p>
          Scoring against the actual rubric, a submission package assembled
          from real plans and receipts, and a clear list of what is still
          missing — the final submission always stays a human action.
        </p>
        <p class="story-status">Planned · judge &amp; submission</p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
